then my hand has B1 as a terminal. So All Simples not possible. If I play B1, then I still have T1. So not helpful. So All Simples is not possible unless I discard both, which I can't do in one move.\n\nAnother possibility: Fully Concealed Hand (all melds concealed, worth 4 points). Since I have no melds, if I can form a concealed hand, that's 4 points. But I need to win by drawing. So if I play a tile and wait for a draw that completes the hand.\n\nBut I need to have enough points. Let's say I play a tile, and then draw the 14th. For example, if I play B3, then my hand is:\n\nW1, W3, T1, T2, T5, T6, T7, B1, B2, B4 (2), B7 (2). Let's see if this can form a winning hand. Let's check possible combinations.\n\nPossible chows:\n\n- T5-6-7\n- B1-2-3 (but I discarded B3, so no)\n- T1-2-3 (no T3)\n- B4-4-4 (if I get one more B4)\n- W1-2-3 (no W2)\n- B7-7-7 (if I get another B7)\n\nSo if I draw a B4, I can form a pung of B4, and then have:\n\nMelds: [T5-6-7, B4-4-4], and then need two more melds. Let's see. The remaining tiles are W1, W3, T1, T2, B1, B2, B7 (2). Maybe form T1-2-3 (needs T3), or W1-2-3 (needs W2). Not possible. So maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, if I draw a B7, then I can form a pung of B7 (since I have two). Then the melds would be [T5-6-7, B7-7-7], and need two more. Still need two more. Maybe form T1-2-3 (no T3) or W1-2-3 (no W2). Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, if I draw a T3, then I can form T1-2-3. But there are 4 T3s left. So possible. If I play B3, and then draw T3, I can form T1-2-3, T5-6-7, and maybe another chow. Let's see. Then the hand would have:\n\nMelds: [T1-2-3, T5-6-7], and then W1, W3, B1, B2, B4 (2), B7 (2). Maybe form B1-2-3 (needs B3, which was discarded). No. So maybe not.\n\nThis seems complicated. Let's consider another play. Suppose I play B7. Then my hand has B1, B2, B3, B4 (2), and W1, W3, T1, T2, T5, T6, T7. If I draw a B4, I can form a pung of B4. Then melds would be [T5-6-7, B4-4-4], and need two more. The remaining tiles: W1, W3, T1, T2, B1, B2, B3. Maybe form B1-2-3 as a chow. Yes! So then:\n\nMelds: [T5-6-7, B4-4-4, B1-2-3], and then W1, W3, T1, T2. Need one more meld. Maybe W1-2-3 (needs W2) or T1-2-3 (needs T3). If I can get T3, then T1-2-3. So if I play B7, and then draw B4 and T3, I can form four chows and a pair. That would be All Chows (24 points), plus Fully Concealed (4 points), and maybe other patterns like All Simples (2 points). Total points would be 24+4+2=30, which is over 8, so valid.\n\nThis seems promising. So playing B7 allows for the possibility of forming multiple chows if the right tiles are drawn. Let's check the steps again.\n\nAfter playing B7:\n\nHand tiles: [W1, W3, T1, T2, T5, T6, T7, B1, B2, B3, B4 (2)].\n\nIf I draw B4, form pung of B4. Now, melds: [B4-4-4, T5-6-7]. Remaining tiles: W1, W3, T1, T2, B1, B2, B3.\n\nThen, form B1-2-3 as a chow. Melds: [B4-4-4, T5-6-7, B1-2-3]. Remaining tiles: W1, W3, T1, T2. Need one more meld. If I can get T3, form T1-2-3. Then four chows and a pair (W1 and W3). That's four chows and a pair. So All Chows (24), Fully Concealed (4), All Simples (2), and maybe others. Total 30 points. That's a good hand.\n\nAlternatively, if I play B7 and draw T3, then I can form T1-2-3. Then melds: [T1-2-3, T5-6-7], and need two more. If I can get B4 and form B4-4-4, and B1-2-3. So same as above.\n\nSo playing B7 seems to open up the possibility of forming a high-scoring hand with All Chows and other patterns.\n\nOther possible plays: For example, playing B3. Then, if I draw B3, I can form a pung. But then I lose the B1-2-3 chow unless I have B3. But if I play B3 now, I can't form that chow. So maybe not as good.\n\nAnother option: playing B4. If I play B4, then I have one B4 left. If I can draw another B4, then form a pair. But not as helpful for chows.\n\nSo, considering the above, playing B7 seems to be the best move to set up for forming multiple chows and a high-scoring hand.\n</think>\n\nEvaluation: Playing B7 allows the possibility of forming multiple chows and a high-scoring hand if the right tiles are drawn.  \nReason: By discarding B7, the hand can potentially form four chows (T5-6-7, B1-2-3, T1-2-3, and another chow) with a pair, leading to All Chows (24 points) and other patterns like Fully Concealed (4 points) and All Simples (2 points).  \nAnswer: Play B7"}
