h. Unless they can form a pair elsewhere. Maybe the W3 is a single, and they need another W3. But that's less likely. \\n\\nAlternatively, if they play T2 and wait for T3, forming T1-T2-T3. Then they have B1-B3, T1-T3, T5-T7. That would be three Chis in different suits for 1-2-3, 5-6-7, and 1-2-3. Wait, but the T1-T2-T3 is 1-2-3 in T, and B1-B2-B3 is 1-2-3 in B. Then T5-T6-T7 is 5-6-7 in T. That would not form the required sequences for Mixed Straight, which needs 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9 in different suits. So in this case, they would have two 1-2-3 Chis and one 5-6-7. That doesn't complete the Mixed Straight. So that wouldn't work. \\n\\nWait, the Mixed Straight requires one of each sequence in different suits. So if they have B1-B3 (1-2-3 in B), T5-T7 (5-6-7 in T), and need a 4-5-6 in another suit. So if they can form a 4-5-6 in W or T. They have W5 and W6 in the unshown tiles (W5:4, W6:4). But they don't have any W4 or W5 in their hand. They have W3. So maybe not. Alternatively, in the B suit, if they get B4-B5-B6, that's 4-5-6 in B. Then they have B1-B3 (1-2-3), B4-B6 (4-5-6), and T5-T7 (5-6-7). That would make three Chis across different sequences. But the T5-T7 is 5-6-7, which is part of the 5-6-7 sequence. So combined with the B4-B6, which is 4-5-6, that would cover 4-5-6-7. But the Mixed Straight requires 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9. They have 1-2-3 and 4-5-6, but not 7-8-9. They have T5-T7 (5-6-7), but not 7-8-9. So that's not enough. \\n\\nHmm, maybe I'm overcomplicating. The main point is to maximize the score. If they can form another Chi in B4-B5-B6, that would give them three Chis, which is All Chows (2 points). Plus if they can form a pair. Alternatively, if they can form a T1-T2-T3 Chi, that would also be three Chis. \\n\\nLooking at the valid actions, playing B5 would allow for the possibility of B4-B5-B6. The unshown B6 has 2 copies, so it's possible. Playing T2 would allow for T1-T2-T3, but the unshown T3 has 4 copies. \\n\\nWhich is better? If they play B5, 